[ Jim Crow laws ](/wiki/Jim_Crow_laws "Jim Crow laws") were introduced after
the passage of the [ Thirteenth Amendment
](/wiki/Thirteenth_Amendment_to_the_United_States_Constitution "Thirteenth
Amendment to the United States Constitution") abolishing [ slavery
](/wiki/Slavery_in_the_United_States "Slavery in the United States") in
America, [ racial discrimination ](/wiki/Racism_in_the_United_States "Racism
in the United States") . The laws mandated strict segregation of the races.
Though many of these laws were passed shortly after the [ Civil War
](/wiki/American_Civil_War "American Civil War") ended, they only became
formalized after the end of the [ Reconstruction era
](/wiki/Reconstruction_era_of_the_United_States "Reconstruction era of the
United States") in 1877. The period that followed the Reconstruction era is
known as the [ nadir of American race relations
](/wiki/Nadir_of_American_race_relations "Nadir of American race relations") .

