  Neural control of muscle force  [  [ edit
](/w/index.php?title=Motor_control&action=edit&section=1 "Edit section: Neural
control of muscle force") ]

All movements, e.g. touching your nose, require [ motor neurons
](/wiki/Motor_neuron "Motor neuron") to fire [ action potentials
](/wiki/Action_potential "Action potential") that results in contraction of [
muscles ](/wiki/Muscle "Muscle") . In humans, ~150,000 motor neurons control
the contraction of ~600 muscles. To produce movements, a subset of 600 muscles
must contract in a temporally precise pattern to produce the right force at
the right time.  [6]

